On Monday, April 17th, 2023 at 11:59 PM, marcuslop...@gmail.com <marcuslop...@gmail.com> wrote:

> My response was not clear, sorry.
> I was referring to the documentation provided in the Verisense-toolbox github. The readme has the following instruction:
>
> "Ensure that do.parallel is set to False in the GGIR entry function"
> https://github.com/ShimmerEngineering/Verisense-Toolbox/tree/master/Verisense_step_algorithm
>
> Marcus
> Em sábado, 15 de abril de 2023 às 08:26:46 UTC-4, Vincent van Hees escreveu:
> > > 1. I included the Verisense step algorithm as an additional function. The function documentation recommended to avoid using the "do.parallel=TRUE" argument. So I processed part1 and part2 with "do.parallel=FALSE" and now I am processing part3-5 with "do.parallel=TRUE". The processing is not being paused due to errors, thank you for the suggestion.
> >
> > The function documentation does not say that. It only says that for external Python functions, but you are not using an external Python function.
> >
> >
> > > 2. The alphabetical order of files within the "meta/ms3.out" is different from the processing order (e.g., the file 9 is followed by file 10 in windows, but by file 91 in GGIR). Apparently, the GGIR requires zeros at the left to order as Windows. I will look for an alternative for ordering the same way in windows.
> >
> > This should be fixed in the recent GGIR 2.9-0 release.
> >
> > Vincent
